menu "BYOK SD-card updater"

    config BYOK_ENABLE_SD_UPDATER
        bool "Scan /SDCARD/Updates/BYOK.tar at boot (stock-compatible SD updater)"
        default y
        help
            Mirrors the stock firmware's own SD `Updates/` install mechanism
            (docs/flash-layout-and-updater.md, D-011, D-015) closely enough that
            dropping the pristine stock BYOK.tar back on the card and
            power-cycling always works, regardless of which app (stock or
            ours) currently boots -- see components/byok_sd_updater's own
            header comment for the full step-by-step and the safety gating.

            With this on (the default), every boot mounts the SD card (if
            present and DOWN is not held) and checks for the update tar;
            with it off, byok_sd_updater_check_and_run() is a no-op and the
            SD card is never touched by this component at all.

            NOTE: this only controls whether the scan/validate path runs.
            The actual OTA flash write it can trigger is a SEPARATE gate,
            CONFIG_BYOK_SD_UPDATER_ARMED (below) -- see that option's help
            text and byok_sd_updater.h for why the two are deliberately not
            the same flag.

    config BYOK_SD_UPDATER_ARMED
        bool "Arm the SD updater's OTA write (esp_ota_begin/write/end + esp_ota_set_boot_partition)"
        default n
        depends on BYOK_ENABLE_SD_UPDATER
        help
            Decision record: docs/design-rationale.md D-017.

            With this ON, a validated `/SDCARD/Updates/BYOK.tar` ->
            `BYOK.bin` member is actually installed: esp_ota_begin() into
            esp_ota_get_next_update_partition(NULL), streamed esp_ota_write(),
            esp_ota_end() (which validates the image -- bad checksum/hash
            fails here, before anything is selected to boot), then
            esp_ota_set_boot_partition() and a reboot. This reproduces
            exactly the STOCK updater's own operation on this device (the
            maker-documented recovery path, D-011/D-015) -- not a new or
            different write, the same one the stock app already performs
            with the same tar format at the same call sequence.

            This is a SEPARATE gate from CONFIG_BYOK_ALLOW_OTADATA_WRITE
            (main/Kconfig.projbuild): that flag continues to gate
            BOOT_ORIGINAL (the protocol command), the boot-time EXECUTE-
            held-3s hatch, and esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback() --
            none of those are affected by this option, and this option
            does not affect them. D-017 arms the SD-updater OTA write on
            its own gate while deliberately leaving those three behind
            CONFIG_BYOK_ALLOW_OTADATA_WRITE=n until the SD path has
            actually been exercised on the device.

            With this OFF (the default), every step through image
            validation still runs and is logged (target partition, target
            size), but the write itself is skipped -- the log says what it
            "would install" -- and no esp_ota_* call is made. The tar is
            left on the card either way in this case (nothing is deleted
            unless a write actually happens -- see byok_sd_updater.h step 7).

            NOTE: firmware/s3/sdkconfig.defaults overrides this option's
            default to `y` for this PoC build (docs/design-rationale.md D-017) --
            `idf.py build` in this tree therefore produces an ARMED image
            even though `n` remains this Kconfig entry's own default.

            Arming this means every boot with a `BYOK.tar` present on the
            card erases and rewrites whatever the OTHER OTA slot currently
            holds -- there is no confirmation step and no on-glass
            indication (the status callback is log-only; this runs before
            byok_display_init()), only the DOWN-hold as an opt-out. This is
            exact parity with the stock updater, which has no target-slot
            content guard either (docs/design-rationale.md D-019); recovery of an
            overwritten OTA slot is always possible by re-applying the
            corresponding vendor tar or our own tar.

            Never arms a write to `factory`, `nvs`, `phy_init`, the
            bootloader, or the partition table --
            esp_ota_get_next_update_partition() can only ever return an
            OTA app slot by construction, and install_member() additionally
            asserts the returned partition's type/subtype before writing
            anything. A corrupt/mismatched image is refused by
            esp_ota_end()'s own validation (or by this component's own
            size/magic/chip_id checks) before esp_ota_set_boot_partition()
            is ever called, and CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=n
            means esp_ota_begin() never touches the other otadata sector
            (docs/bootloader-analysis.md). `esptool erase_region 0x910000
            0x2000` remains the ultimate off-device fallback regardless of
            this flag's value.

endmenu
